            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Please attempt to write a commit message..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Meis",
                        "email": "christopher.meis@9elements.com",
                        "username": "ChriMarMe"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This set of configs doesn't seem nearly descriptive enough to be able to monitor a VR, and we have other VRs in the tree already, so I'm a little confused what this is for, or how it relates to the existing VRs.  That confusion isn't helped by the fact that there's no commit message."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "to be specific:\n1. There's one set of thresholds.  VRs generally have many sensors.\n2. The type itself \"VoltageRegulator\" implies that there's some standard for accessing VRs, which to my understanding there isn't.  If there is, please name it the name of the spec (as we've done with other types like pmbus)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Meis",
                        "email": "christopher.meis@9elements.com",
                        "username": "ChriMarMe"
                    },
                    "message": "Thank you for your review.\n\nWhat do you consider \"descriptive enough\"? I looked at some VR definitions in config and derived some common fields from that as a first step to create the schema. By \"VRs in the tree\" I can only guess you mean the configuration-files, but we lack a common template(schema) for VRs. In the future we want to update VRs over I2C with the new update flow from https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/master/designs/code-update.md\nThere I want to implement device (VR) specific classes with the update specifics for each device, but with a common interface for providing the binary file and executing the update on the chip, taking care of pre and post conditions etc.\n\nThe schema can serve as a template or guideline for adding VRs to config files. Details in the schema can describe, if it's updateable with the flow mentioned above or not. \n\nRegarding 1.\n\nThe definition of thresholds from `legacy.json` is already of type array as shown below. My schema also contains the property \"Labels\", which can map a specific threshold to a label. (This is done in config files already)\n\n```\n\"Thresholds\": {\n                \"items\": {\n                    \"properties\": {\n                        \"Direction\": {\n                            \"type\": \"string\"\n                        },\n                        \"Hysteresis\": {\n                            \"type\": \"number\"\n                        },\n                        \"Label\": {\n                            \"type\": \"string\"\n                        },\n                        \"Name\": {\n                            \"type\": \"string\"\n                        },\n                        \"Severity\": {\n                            \"type\": \"number\",\n                            \"minimum\": 0,\n                            \"maximum\": 4\n                        },\n                        \"Value\": {\n                            \"type\": \"number\"\n                        }\n                    },\n                    \"required\": [\"Direction\", \"Name\", \"Severity\", \"Value\"],\n                    \"type\": \"object\"\n                },\n                \"type\": \"array\"\n            },\n\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> What do you consider \"descriptive enough\"?\n\nThe information that software, with no prior knowledge can go access the deveice.  For VRs that tends to be a bus an address and a model (which is put into type).\n\nIn this model you've invented a new type, called \"VR\" that doesn't give an indication of how one would communicate with the VR"
                }
            ],
